<section id="A1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.4 </span>Results on RULER</h3>

<div id="A1.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 Main Results ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Judge Q: Trainable Queries for Optimized Information Retentionin KV Cache Eviction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>, we provide the performance results for sequence lengths of 8k and 32k.
Additionally, we test the effect of different methods under input length is 16k.
The experimental results are shown in Table <a href="#A1.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ A.4 Results on RULER ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Judge Q: Trainable Queries for Optimized Information Retentionin KV Cache Eviction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>.
When the sequence length is 16k, our method consistently outperforms the baselines by at least 5 points across multiple budgets, and in some cases by more than 10 points.
All the experimental results strongly demonstrate the effectiveness and generalization of Judge Q.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
